A||N||29||RoadRocket||Best "Bolt-on" engines.||||5||
Z||000000||RoadRocket||07-22-2001||01:19 PM||||So what are the best engine options for a Fiero that will go in with the least amount of hassle.<P>I'm looking to keep my Fiero going for a long time, I'm told my engine has another 75k on it but am wondering what my options are.<P>I've played with the idea of a V8 Archie, but it is a little more than I'd like to pay.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fropuff||07-22-2001||03:49 PM||||I'd definately say a 3.4L pushrod from any '93-'95 F-Body.  It's probably the largest engine that can go in without a lot of trouble.  You don't have to change wiring or anything, just a few minor things; starter mounting, drilling a few holes, grinding a little (for automatic transmission), etc.  You can bolt any Fiero transmission to it as well.  Since it's also a 60 degree 6-cylinder, you can use all the 2.8L "accessories", and make it look factory.  I plan on doing that this winter.  Just do a search for "3.4", and you'll come up with days of posts to get more information.    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||07-22-2001||03:50 PM||||I'm sorry; I see that you have the 2.5L Tech 4 engine in yours.  You can still do a 3.4L swap, it's just harder.  Any 6-cylinder swap will be hard to do anyway.  The easiest would be another 4-cylinder.  Sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RoadRocket||07-22-2001||05:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>I'm sorry; I see that you have the 2.5L Tech 4 engine in yours.  You can still do a 3.4L swap, it's just harder.  Any 6-cylinder swap will be hard to do anyway.  The easiest would be another 4-cylinder.  Sorry.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's OK.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>I said "the engines that would go in with the least amount of hassle. I suspect the 3.4L swap you're suggesting would go in with less hassle that the V8 swap. It would also be less expensive.<P>Which is what I wanted to know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||07-22-2001||06:32 PM||||The only other 4 Cyl that people put in Fieros is a Quad 4. Since the Quad 4 needs to be swapped in with a Quad 4 transmission, different mounts, and it needs rewiring, it's just as hard as a v6 swap. <P>Other options, all with theiir own hassles are:<BR>2.8 or 3.1, probably the easiest since you can get it to work with Fiero based parts.<BR>3.4, in it's own section since you have to drill the starter holes and other minor things.<BR>Small block, you already ruled this one out, though it could be easier than a 2.8 swap if you keep it simple.<BR>3.8, More complicated if you have a manual trans, you also need to (pay someone to) reworkthe wiring harness.<BR>4.1-4.6-4,9 caddilac. Harder than a 3.8 if if you run into fuel injection problems with the Cadillac computer.<BR>Quad 4, already covered, can be easier than the 3.8 depending again on the transmission.<BR>Northstar, costs alot, wiring hassles, computer hassles, Manual transmission hassles.<P>Additions? Corrections?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||07-22-2001||06:35 PM||||I forgot the 3.4 TDC<BR>I'd rate that as slightly easier than a 3.8 Since there shouldn't be any problems finding a flywheel for a manual transmission.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||AutoXer||07-22-2001||08:34 PM||||There is one more that a lot of people don't think of.<P>A 2.0L turbo out of a late 80's sunbird.<P>You'll end up with a 165 HP engine with-out any engine mods and your car will have very nearly 50/50 weight distribution (sweet handling).<P>Before the flames start let me make it clear that I would not recommend this option.  <P>It's not that the instillation would be that hard.  Should be about the same as a V6 swap and easier than a quad-4.  the problem will be that the 2.0 turbo was finicky at best and will become increasingly harder to find parts for.<P>(My $0.02)||||reg||3
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||07-22-2001||09:59 PM||||Yeah, I'm not a fan of turbo 2's myself.<BR>I'm kind of biased though since one tried to kill me.  (If I wouldn't have moved before the motor blew apart...)<BR>It would still be about as hard as a Q4 because you have to swap the tranny too if i remember right.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||07-22-2001||10:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AutoXer:<BR><B>There is one more that a lot of people don't think of.<P>A 2.0L turbo out of a late 80's sunbird.<P>You'll end up with a 165 HP engine with-out any engine mods and your car will have very nearly 50/50 weight distribution (sweet handling).<P>Before the flames start let me make it clear that I would not recommend this option.  <P>It's not that the instillation would be that hard.  Should be about the same as a V6 swap and easier than a quad-4.  the problem will be that the 2.0 turbo was finicky at best and will become increasingly harder to find parts for.<P>(My $0.02)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Those turbo sunbirds are pretty quick. One was behind me turning onto the main drive the other day. I turned and got into it a little bit, and she turned and came flying by me like I was standing still.||||reg||1
Z||000009||underdog||07-23-2001||03:10 AM||||can anyone give any info or websites on swapping a 3.1? anyone done it here? ||||reg||10
Z||000010||AutoXer||07-23-2001||05:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>It would still be about as hard as a Q4 because you have to swap the tranny too if i remember right.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why the required tranny swap?  Don't they use the same Getrag/Muncie 5-speed?||||reg||5
Z||000011||Formula||07-23-2001||11:13 AM||||or a 4 cyl I would say the carbed 4.1-4.9 would be easiest, your going to have to change everything for the v6 anyway so why not go v8 right.<P>for v-6 to v-6, the rwd 3.1-3.4 isnt very hard to do, it would be a lot easier to run the fuel injection though.  I have a rwd 3.2 stroker in mine now and it took about a month to swap in and tune, Im running a holley 390 carb and edelbrock torker 2 intake though so tuning wasnt very fun. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula||07-23-2001||11:14 AM||||btw.. you dont need an archie kit for the 4.1-4.9 carbed caddy motor, I think you need a hundred dollar mount and have to be creative for the pulleys.||||reg||1
Z||000013||PBJ||07-23-2001||11:24 AM||||all you need to know about a 4.X caddy swap.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||RoadRocket||07-23-2001||11:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>or a 4 cyl I would say the carbed 4.1-4.9 would be easiest, your going to have to change everything for the v6 anyway so why not go v8 right.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I read elsewhere about how the v8 affects handling. Handling is one of the things that I love about the little car. That, and the heavy price tag are keeping me away from a v8 solution.<P>We'll see, though. I just might change my mind when I win the lottery.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||07-24-2001||01:06 AM||||3.4TDC<BR>Uses stock mounts(replaced with urethane)<P>Bolts to stock trans with basically the stock flywheel(nuetral balanced)<P>Uses stock throttle cable w/end altered<P>Fits perfectly into compartment with only slight alterations<P>Gain ten+ years of tech,forged goodies.<P>Close to same weight as stock motor<P>Doesn't have so much torque as bigger supercharged motors,so your clutch/trans should last longer,yet revs very fast.<P>Sounds incredible.<P>I put one in in a month of one day events using very common tools/parts,never had to trial fit the engine/trans assy.,just put it in,the most challenging part was getting the wiring diagram for the engine and wiring the whole thing(a week of a couple hours a day).<BR>The fun part was making my own stainless exhaust by fitting all the pieces together,then having it welded.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Pontiaddict||07-24-2001||01:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AutoXer:<BR><B> Why the required tranny swap?  Don't they use the same Getrag/Muncie 5-speed?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If I remember correctly, they have a different bolt pattern. Like the q4 does.<BR>Maybe I'm thinking of something else, I really havent bothered reasearching them since I think they're junk.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Pontiaddict||07-24-2001||01:37 AM||||I'm not saying the 2.0 and the quad 4 have the same bolt pattern. Just reread what was written and it kind of sounded like i said that.||||reg||1
