Z||000023||lowCG||07-25-2001||01:03 AM||||The engine comes pretty well setup tech-wise,so after tweaking my own engines for the last twenty years,I decided to leave it to Detroit for a change.I don't think you'd get much more power from this motor without changing out the cams,and it runs great,so why bother(?).<BR>West Coast Fiero's site is a good checklist for a starting point,and I'd suggest using the pre '95 motors if possible.<BR>The ignition is controlled by the ECU;no distributor.<BR>The coolant crossover is gone now;no need as the water outlets are actually in the right place on this motor,unlike the 2.8 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||saleenfiero||07-25-2001||01:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RoadRocket:<BR><B> Tell me more.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>SD4 was a $10k race engine with nil parts availabilty and worse reliability, horsepower out the ass and from what I heard crappy torque band||||reg||1
Z||000025||Will||07-25-2001||03:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RoadRocket:<BR><B> Tell me more.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This thread has some pictures of it: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012269.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012269.html</A> <P>I've never heard from a reliable source that these engines were not reliable.<BR>Probably $10-12K for the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Will||07-25-2001||03:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>The engine comes pretty well setup tech-wise,so after tweaking my own engines for the last twenty years,I decided to leave it to Detroit for a change.I don't think you'd get much more power from this motor without changing out the cams,and it runs great,so why bother(?).<BR>West Coast Fiero's site is a good checklist for a starting point,and I'd suggest using the pre '95 motors if possible.<BR>The ignition is controlled by the ECU;no distributor.<BR>The coolant crossover is gone now;no need as the water outlets are actually in the right place on this motor,unlike the 2.8  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't the '96 engines have larger ports and intake runners?||||reg||1
Z||000027||lowCG||07-26-2001||01:02 AM||||The last years had a slight increase in upper mid/top end power with the larger intake,but I'm sure the tradeoff that the early motors offer with the increase in lower/mid torque will be a wash,and the computers on the earlier cars are much easier to tune for several reasons.<BR>I'm sure the early motor with an extrude hone job on the intake would run great if not better than my motor.<BR>Maybe someday I'll get with the program and get an early ECM to get the last few tuning adjustments I want(up the rev limit,bump up the timing on the lower revs,and fix the wandering idle)and have the car run perfectly.||||reg||1
Z||000028||RoadRocket||07-27-2001||12:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by saleenfiero:<BR><B> SD4 was a $10k race engine with nil parts availabilty and worse reliability, horsepower out the ass and from what I heard crappy torque band</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>At that price, I think I'd settle for a V8.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
